================================================================================
HOOKMANAGER KNOWLEDGE BASE
================================================================================
Files:  HookManager.h / HookManager.cpp / dllmain.cpp
Purpose: Single point of entry for all MinHook function hooks in the mod.
         DllMain delegates entirely to HookManager. Individual modules
         (Randomizer.cpp etc.) declare their detour/original pairs but have
         no knowledge of MinHook or installation order.
================================================================================


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DESIGN INTENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Responsibilities are split across three files:

    dllmain.cpp
        Knows nothing about hooks or mod logic.
        Calls HookManager::InstallAll on attach, RemoveAll on detach.

    HookManager.cpp
        Knows which functions are hooked and how to install them.
        Does not know what the hooks do.
        Adding a new hook = one new Add() call in RegisterHooks(). Nothing else.

    Randomizer.cpp (and any future module)
        Defines what hooks do — the detour functions and original pointers.
        Has no knowledge of MinHook or installation order.

Load sequence:
    DllMain → HookManager::InstallAll()
                  → MH_Initialize()
                  → RegisterHooks()
                       → Add(RVA_LOAD_CARS,  Hook_LoadCars,  &Orig_LoadCars)
                       → Add(RVA_BUILD_GRID, Hook_BuildGrid, &Orig_BuildGrid)

Unload sequence:
    DllMain → HookManager::RemoveAll()
                  → MH_DisableHook() for each recorded entry
                  → MH_Uninitialize()


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUBLIC API
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

HookManager::InstallAll() → bool
    Initialises MinHook and installs every hook in RegisterHooks().
    Called once from DllMain on DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH.
    Returns false only if MinHook itself fails to initialize.
    Individual hook failures are non-fatal — logged and skipped.

HookManager::RemoveAll()
    Disables every installed hook and shuts down MinHook.
    Called once from DllMain on DLL_PROCESS_DETACH.

HookManager::Add(target, detour, original, name) → bool
    Hooks one function. Used internally by RegisterHooks().
    Can also be called directly if a hook needs to be added outside the
    normal startup sequence, though this is discouraged.

    target   — absolute runtime address of the function to hook
               (produced by AbsFromRva(RVA_CONSTANT))
    detour   — your replacement function; must match calling convention
               and signature of the original
    original — pointer to the trampoline; call this from inside the detour
               to invoke the real original function
    name     — label used only in debug output (OutputDebugStringA)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HookManager::Add — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Guard against a zero address
    if (!target) { log; return false; }

    If AbsFromRva returned zero the RVA constant is wrong or the module base
    was not found. Nothing to hook. Bail immediately rather than passing a null
    pointer to MinHook which would crash.

Step 2 — Create the hook (MH_CreateHook)
    MH_CreateHook(target, detour, original)

    MinHook reads the machine code at target, copies the first few instructions
    into a small trampoline buffer, then prepares a jump from target to detour.
    Writes the trampoline address into *original so the detour can call through
    to the real function.

    At this point the hook EXISTS but is NOT yet active. The original function
    bytes have not been modified.

Step 3 — Enable the hook (MH_EnableHook)
    MH_EnableHook(target)

    This is when the patch goes live. MinHook overwrites the first bytes of the
    original function with a jump to the detour. From this point on, any call
    to that function inside RVGL executes the detour instead.

Step 4 — Record the entry
    g_hooks.push_back({ target, detour, original, name, true })

    Appends to the internal vector so RemoveAll can find and disable this hook
    later. The name field is only used for debug output.

Failure handling:
    MH_ERROR_ALREADY_CREATED and MH_ERROR_ENABLED are accepted as non-failures
    (idempotent — the hook is already in the desired state).
    Any other error is logged via OutputDebugStringA and returns false.
    The caller (RegisterHooks / InstallAll) treats this as non-fatal and
    continues installing remaining hooks.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RegisterHooks — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    static void RegisterHooks()

The complete, authoritative list of every function the mod intercepts.
This is the ONLY place that needs to change when adding or removing a hook.

Each entry follows this pattern:

    HookManager::Add(
        AbsFromRva(RVA_LOAD_CARS),                             // where to hook
        reinterpret_cast<void*>(Randomizer::Hook_LoadCars),    // run this instead
        reinterpret_cast<void**>(&Randomizer::Orig_LoadCars),  // trampoline out
        "LoadAllCars"                                          // debug label
    );

AbsFromRva converts the compile-time RVA constant from Addresses.h into the
actual runtime address by adding the RVGL module base. This is done at call
time (not compile time) because the module base is only known once the
process is running.

To add a new hook to the mod:
    1. Define Hook_Foo and Orig_Foo in your module (e.g. Randomizer.cpp).
    2. Add one Add() call here.
    3. Add the RVA constant to Addresses.h.
    Nothing else changes.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HookManager::InstallAll — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Initialise MinHook
    MH_Initialize()

    Allocates MinHook's internal heap and trampoline buffer pool.
    Must succeed before any MH_CreateHook calls.
    MH_ERROR_ALREADY_INITIALIZED is accepted as non-failure (another DLL in the
    same process may have already initialised MinHook).

    On genuine failure: log and return false immediately. No hooks are installed.

Step 2 — Register all hooks
    RegisterHooks()

    Runs every Add() call in sequence. Individual failures are logged but do
    not abort the remaining hooks. A partially working mod is more useful than
    a mod that refuses to load because one hook failed.

Step 3 — Report and return
    return installed > 0

    Returns false only if zero hooks were installed (fundamental problem).
    Returns true as long as at least one hook is active.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HookManager::RemoveAll — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Guard
    if (!g_minHookInitialized) return;

    If InstallAll never succeeded there is nothing to clean up. Prevents
    calling MinHook functions on an uninitialized state.

Step 2 — Disable every hook
    for (entry : g_hooks) MH_DisableHook(entry.target)

    Restores the original bytes at each hooked function. After this, all RVGL
    functions execute their original code again.

    IMPORTANT: This must happen before MH_Uninitialize. MinHook frees the
    trampoline buffers during Uninitialize. If hooks were still active, RVGL
    code jumping into a freed trampoline would crash.

Step 3 — Clear the recorded list
    g_hooks.clear()

    Drops all entries. The vector releases its memory.

Step 4 — Shut down MinHook
    MH_Uninitialize()

    Frees MinHook's internal heap and trampoline buffer pool. Safe to unload
    the DLL after this returns.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DllMain — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Intentionally minimal. All logic is delegated to HookManager.

DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH:

    DisableThreadLibraryCalls(hModule)
        Prevents DllMain from being called again on thread create/destroy.
        We have no per-thread initialisation; every unnecessary DllMain call
        is a small risk, so we opt out entirely.

    Sleep(50)
        Short wait after the DLL loads. With suspended-process injection
        this is not strictly necessary — RVGL's main thread hasn't run yet.
        Left in as a safety margin for edge cases on slower machines or
        unusual process startup sequences.

    HookManager::InstallAll()
        The only real work done on attach.

DLL_PROCESS_DETACH:

    HookManager::RemoveAll()
        The only real work done on detach.
        Fires when RVGL exits or the DLL is explicitly unloaded.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MINIHOOK CONCEPTS REFERENCED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Trampoline
    A small buffer allocated by MinHook that holds a copy of the original
    function's first few instructions (the ones overwritten by the hook jump),
    followed by a jump back into the original function after those bytes.
    Calling through the trampoline (via *original) therefore executes the
    real original code as if the hook were not there.

MH_CreateHook vs MH_EnableHook
    CreateHook = prepare the hook (allocate trampoline, do not patch yet).
    EnableHook = write the jump instruction (patch goes live).
    Separating these allows hooks to be created ahead of time and enabled
    atomically, though we don't exploit that here.

Hook disable before Uninitialize
    MH_Uninitialize frees the trampoline buffer pool. Any hook still pointing
    into that pool would become a dangling pointer. Always disable hooks before
    calling Uninitialize.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OutputDebugStringA
    All logging uses OutputDebugStringA, visible in tools like DebugView or
    the Visual Studio Output window when a debugger is attached. No log files
    are written. To see output: run DebugView as administrator before launching
    the game via the launcher.

Non-fatal hook failures
    A hook failure means the mod simply does not intercept that function.
    RVGL continues running normally for that code path. Whether that is
    acceptable depends on which hook failed — a failed Hook_BuildGrid means
    the randomizer never fires, but the game itself still runs.

g_hooks vector
    Uses std::vector with default allocation. Populated during InstallAll,
    cleared during RemoveAll. Not thread-safe, but DllMain attach/detach
    are single-threaded by the Windows loader lock so this is fine.
